    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Ājīvikas taught niyati, absolute determinism: everything that has happened, is happening and will happen is preordained by cosmic principles, there is no free will, and the karma doctrine is a fallacy.</li><li>Their own scriptures are lost, so everything known of them comes from Buddhist, Jain and other texts, written largely by rivals, and scholars question how fairly these describe them.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>'s inscriptions in the Barabar Caves in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, the oldest surviving cave temples of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, record his gifts to the Ājīvikas; tax inscriptions show the school in southern India until 1346.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In several Barabar Cave inscriptions the word “Ājīvikas” was later chiselled out, probably by religious rivals while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmi-script">Brahmi script</a> was still understood; the original lettering is so deep that it can still be read.</p></aside>
    </section>

