      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 484 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makkhali-gosala">Makkhali Gosala</a>, generally considered the founder of the Ājīvikas, lives as a contemporary of the Buddha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 258 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> dedicates Barabar Caves to the Ājīvikas in the 12th year of his reign; further dedications follow in his 19th year, about 251 BCE</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2nd century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">After the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a> the Ājīvikas find only occasional mention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> literature</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 446</span><span class="rf-text">The Pallava king Simhavarman II is the earliest ruler of a series of southern Indian inscriptions that record a tax on Ājīvikas</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">6th century</span><span class="rf-text">Varahamihira's Brhaj-jataka mentions the Ājīvikas among the major ascetic groups</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1292</span><span class="rf-text">The Jain commentator Mallisena, writing the Syadvada-manjari, suggests that the Ājīvikas still existed in his time</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1346</span><span class="rf-text">The last dated inscription recording a tax on Ājīvikas or Acuvas is set up near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolar">Kolar</a></span></li></ol>
