    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/makkhali-gosala"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/makkhali-gosala.jpg" alt="Makkhali Gosala" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/makkhali-gosala">Makkhali Gosala</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Four_Scenes_from_the_Life_of_the_Buddha_-_Parinirvana_-_Kushan_dynasty,_late_2nd_to_early_3rd_century_AD,_Gandhara,_schist_-_Freer_Gallery_of_Art_-_DSC05119.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Daderot, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Buddhist and Jain texts name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makkhali-gosala">Makkhali Gosala</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> Gośala Maskariputra) as a contemporary of the Buddha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a>, and the Ājīvikas are generally held to have begun in the 5th century BCE, although their exact origins are unknown. In the Sandaka Sutta they are said to recognise three emancipators: Nanda Vaccha, Kisa Saṅkicca and Makkhali Gosāla. Gosala is generally considered the founder of the movement, but some sources state that he led a large congregation of ascetics without founding it, and Jarl Charpentier and others suggest that the tradition existed well before his birth.</p><p>The Jain Bhagavati Sutra relates that he was born at Saravana, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shravasti">Savatthi</a>, to Mankha, a professional mendicant, and his mother Bhaddā; his name Gosala means “cowshed” and refers to his humble birthplace. According to the same text he was for six years a disciple of Mahavira, after which the two quarrelled and parted. Gosala argued that the Gosala who had been Mahavira's companion had died and that a different soul now inhabited his body, which Mahavira called sophistry. The Bhagavati Sutra also mentions a debate and then “coming to blows” between the two factions.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The distinctive doctrine of the Ājīvikas was niyati, “fate”. According to Jain and Buddhist texts they held that everything in human life and the universe is preordained by cosmic principles, that true choice does not exist, and that religious or ethical practice therefore has no effect on one's future. They regarded the karma theory as a fallacy. James Lochtefeld compares the universe to a ball of wrapped string that unrolls until it is done and then goes no further. Riepe notes that this did not make them pessimists: as with Calvinists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, he calls them optimists. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makkhali-gosala">Makkhali Gosala</a> seems to have combined niyati with svabhava (nature) and sangati (change), and one reading holds that he thought some future events, such as salvation for all, to be strictly determined.</p><p>The Ājīvikas were considered atheists and presumed no creator deity. They held that every being has a soul (ātman), which passes through many births and progresses towards its predestined nirvana, and that the soul has a material form. Their atomism held that all things are made of atoms whose aggregation is governed by cosmic rules. Buddhist texts report an antinomian ethics, that there are no objective moral laws, yet both Jain and Buddhist records say the Ājīvikas lived a simple ascetic life without clothes or possessions. Tamil literature suggests they practised non-violence and vegetarianism.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahavira"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahavira.jpg" alt="Mahavira" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahavir.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dayodaya, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ājīvikas are said to have had a fully elaborate philosophy produced by their scholars and logicians, but those texts are lost, and the original scriptures are probably gone. The primary texts are said to have included the ten Purvas (eight Mahanimittas and two Maggas) and the Onpatu Katir. The Mahanimittas claim that the Bhagavati Sutra was drawn from the teachings Gosala received from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a> when he was his disciple, and they gave long sections to mapping the sun, moon, planets and stars and to astrology and fortune telling.</p><p>What is known comes from secondary sources: inconsistent fragments in Jain texts such as the Bhagavati Sutra, in Buddhist texts such as the Samaññaphala Sutta, the Sandaka Sutta and Buddhaghosa's commentary on the first of these, and a few mentions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> texts such as the Vayu Purana. Later Tamil works, the Nīlakēci and the Manimekalai among them, also describe Ājīvika views; the Nīlakēci tells of two divinities, Okkali and Ōkali, who instructed men in the scriptures, and three Tamil texts list seven kayas, elements of earth, water, fire, air, joy, sorrow and life. Because these accounts were written by rivals, scholars question whether the school has been fairly summarised, and some suspect deliberate distortion. Paul Dundas doubts that a doctrine denying the efficacy of effort could have grounded a path of renunciation. Johannes Bronkhorst argues that Gosala taught that ascetic practice can stop new karma but not remove old karma.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bindusara"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bindusara.jpg" alt="Bindusara" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bindusara">Bindusara</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lomas_Rishi_entrance.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photo Dharma from Penang, Malaysia, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Buddhist and Jain texts name the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shravasti">Savatthi</a> (Śrāvastī), near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a> in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, as the hub of the Ājīvikas in the 1st millennium BCE. The school spread rapidly across ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangha">Sangha</a> Geham, a community centre, on the island now called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> and a presence in the western state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> by the 4th century BCE, in the age of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>. The Ashokavadana, a legend of the 4th century CE, says that the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bindusara">Bindusara</a> and his chief queen Shubhadrangi followed this philosophy, and the school is held to have reached its peak of popularity in his reign.</p><p>After the decline of the Maurya Empire in the 2nd century BCE the Ājīvikas find only occasional mention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> literature. In northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> they may have become insignificant by the Shunga period, and references in the Vayu Purana suggest that during the Gupta period their practices had changed substantially and the sect was declining rapidly. They continued in the south: at least 17 inscriptions from southern India, from the time of the Pallava king Simhavarman II (c. 446 CE) to the 14th century, record a tax on Ājīvikas or Acuvas, believed to be a Tamil form of the name; the last, of 1346 CE, were found at three places around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolar">Kolar</a>. Inscriptions suggest a significant presence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, especially in Kolar district, and in parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>. A. L. Basham thinks they may have survived there until the 15th century; the 14th-century Sarva-Darshana-Sangraha does not mention them.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ashoka"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashoka.jpg" alt="Ashoka" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashoka%27s_visit_to_the_Ramagrama_stupa_Sanchi_Stupa_1_Southern_gateway.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photo Dharma from Sadao, Thailand, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ājīvikas were organised renunciates who formed discrete communities, though the precise identity of the Ājīvikas and their relationships with other movements are not well understood. The school and the Cārvāka philosophy appealed most to the warrior, industrial and mercantile classes of ancient Indian society. Scholars classify them differently: Riepe calls them a distinct heterodox school, Raju says that Ājīvikas and Cārvākas can be called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>'s inscriptions suggest that he considered them more closely related to the Vedic schools than to Buddhists or Jains.</p><p>The Barabar Caves in Jehanabad district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, are the oldest surviving cave temples of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Their dedications to the Ājīvikas were engraved in the 12th and 19th years of Ashoka's reign, about 258 and 251 BCE. Each cave has a large rectangular hall and a small circular domed chamber, and they were probably used for meditation. The Ashokavadana says that Ashoka, after turning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, ordered the killing of all Ājīvikas in Pundravardhana and that about 18,000 were supposedly executed; the whole story may be apocryphal, and K. T. S. Sarao and Benimadhab Barua call stories of such persecution a fabrication born of sectarian propaganda, since his own inscriptions record generous gifts to the Ājīvikas. Basham holds that any ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> between the Maurya and Gupta periods may have persecuted them.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Several writers suggest that the Ājīvikas influenced other schools. Isaeva suggests that their ideas affected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and various Vedic schools, and Riepe holds that the details of their atomism gave the foundations of later atomist theories in the Jain, Buddhist and Vedic traditions. The Jains, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atomic-theory">atomic theory</a> was closest to theirs, began to claim to have first formulated an Indian atomism only with the later Avisakya Sūtra, and Basham regards the Vaiśeṣika atomism as different from theirs and more complete. Basham also proposes that they may have influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dvaita-vedanta">Dvaita Vedanta</a>.</p><p>The Ājīvikas seem to have faded rather than disappeared at a stroke. Basham notes several cases in which other writers confuse them with other groups: a commentator of the Achara-sara takes them for Buddhists, and in the Neelakesi their leader asks that his followers not be mistaken for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambaras</a>. He concludes that the surviving Ājīvikas may have adopted some beliefs and customs of the more popular faiths and possibly merged with them, and that growing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, Buddhist and Jain influence may have led to their decline. The Barabar Caves were later occupied by Buddhists, Hindus and Muslims, and their carved dedications, with the word Ājīvikas defaced in some places, are the most visible trace of the school.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Ājīvikas were an ancient Indian renunciant movement, traditionally founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makkhali-gosala">Makkhali Gosala</a> in the 5th century BCE, that taught absolute fate and rejected karma. Known only through the texts of Buddhist, Jain and other writers, who were often rivals, they enjoyed Mauryan patronage and survived in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> until inscriptions of the 14th century.</em></p>

